  "observations": [
    "Split was the only action that found new IDs in NW (+5) and it captured every new ID zoom/shift found in NE (+10 total, 3 only split found). But split does not strictly dominate: in SE, zoom_in found 3 IDs that split missed.",
    "Efficiency per search: zoom_in 7 new / 4 searches (1.75/search), shift 5 / 4 (1.25/search), split 18 / 16 (1.125/search). Split gives the largest absolute gain per cell but at 4x the page cost; cheap viewport changes recover part of the same gain in some cells.",
    "Cells that looked finished were not: baselines held only 14-38 in-target IDs (raw rows 89-111), yet actions added +6 (SE), +5 (NW), +10 (NE) IDs, i.e. 13-29% more than one search found. Across the circle, observed IDs rose from 87 to 108.",
    "SW stayed empty under every action (10 searches total across Experiments 1-2, hundreds of raw rows, 0 in-target). That quadrant reads as genuinely sparse for 'restaurant' in Maps returns, and no action should keep spending there without a different keyword or reference check.",
    "Zoom/shift overlap with the baseline is partial by design (they view a different window), so their in-target counts (e.g. NW zoom 22 of baseline 38) understate the baseline set; the compared quantity is new IDs, not rediscovery."
  ],
  "claim": "Action-comparison evidence only. Unions are larger observed sets, not every place in the cell. Total places unknown."
